Two 60 mm fiberglass stages: L1000W booster, K550W sustainer, separating at burnout with a 0.5 s gap before the sustainer lights. It reaches 5,674.8 m, clearing your 5,000 m floor with room.
Both stages land at 3.4 m/s and 3.3 m/s on a 1.8 m and a 2.1 m chute, inside your 4.5 m/s limit. Margin is 1.48 cal at rail exit, and it falls to 0.95 cal during the boost. That stays above zero, so it passes, but the margin is thin while the booster burns.
Expect about 7.6 km of sustainer drift in that 20 km/h wind, so fly it with a tracker.
Caveat: the simulator warned that body calculations may not be entirely accurate at supersonic speeds (max Mach 1.93) and that the booster body is an open forward airframe.
