A 57.5 mm single stage with both D12s in one cluster mount, 0.065 kg of nose ballast and a 0.50 m chute. It reaches 345.7 m, so the 90 m floor is cleared several times over, and lands at 4.6 m/s, inside your 6 m/s limit.
0.252 kg liftoff, 8.4:1 thrust-to-weight, rail exit 21.4 m/s, margin 1.03 cal at launch and through the burn, deployment at 6.7 s.
The wide hull the cluster forces costs fin authority, so the nose weight is doing the stability work. Two motors lit together is something no simulation can promise, and one out means asymmetric thrust.
